const faq = [
  { q: 'What does “no hole card” mean?', a: 'The dealer gets only one card, face up, during the deal. The second card is drawn after all players have acted. If it makes blackjack, the dealer also wins any extra money you put out to double or split. In American games the dealer checks for blackjack first and the hand ends there.' },
  { q: 'Can you surrender in Spanish casinos?', a: 'Usually not. A few tables and some Spanish-licensed online casinos offer it; check the rules sign at the table.' },
  { q: 'Why don’t you double 11 against a 10 in Spain?', a: 'Without a hole card the dealer may still have blackjack. If you double and they do, you lose twice as much. That risk makes doubling 11 against a 10 or ace a losing play, so you just hit.' },
  { q: 'What is the house edge in a Spanish casino?', a: 'With typical rules (six decks, dealer stands on soft 17, no hole card, double on 9–11 only, no surrender, blackjack pays 3:2) and perfect basic strategy, about 0.6%.' },
];
