<Guide lang="en" route="spain" title="Blackjack Rules in Spain (No Hole Card) · Veintiuno" h1="Blackjack rules in Spain" description="Blackjack in Spanish casinos: no hole card, doubling on 9, 10 or 11 only, usually no surrender. Which plays change versus Las Vegas, with the adjusted strategy chart." updated="2026-09-27" readMinutes={6} faq={faq} related={['strategy', 'howToPlay', 'howToWin', 'counting']} wide>
  <div slot="top" class="read">
    <div class="tldr"><p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Spanish casinos deal <strong>no hole card</strong>, allow doubling on <strong>9, 10 or 11 only</strong> and usually offer <strong>no surrender</strong>. That changes several key plays — for example, you don’t double 11 against a 10 or split eights against an ace. Note: this is regular blackjack, not the different game called “Spanish 21”.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div slot="wide" class="read">
    <h2 id="rules">Typical rules in Spain</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Spanish casinos (typical)</th><th>Las Vegas (typical)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Decks</td><td>6 (sometimes 8)</td><td>6 or 8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dealer hole card</td><td><strong>No</strong> (second card dealt last)</td><td>Yes, dealer peeks for blackjack</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dealer on soft 17</td><td>Stands</td><td>Stands or hits, by table</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Doubling</td><td><strong>9, 10 or 11 only</strong></td><td>Any two cards</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Double after split</td><td>Usually yes</td><td>Usually yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Surrender</td><td><strong>Usually no</strong></td><td>Sometimes (late)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blackjack pays</td><td>3 to 2</td><td>3 to 2, or 6 to 5 at cheap tables</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h2 id="chart">Strategy chart for Spanish casinos</h2>
  </div>
  <div slot="wide"><StrategyChart lang="en" preset="spainCasino" /></div>

  <h2 id="changes">Plays that change versus Las Vegas</h2>
  <p>Two rules explain everything. First, with <strong>no hole card</strong> a dealer blackjack takes everything on the table, including doubles and splits — so you don’t want extra money out against a 10 or ace:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>11 vs 10 or ace:</strong> hit, don’t double.</li>
    <li><strong>A-A vs ace:</strong> hit, don’t split.</li>
    <li><strong>8-8 vs 10 or ace:</strong> hit (or surrender if offered). In Las Vegas you always split them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Second, <strong>doubling only on 9–11</strong> removes every soft double:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A-2 to A-6:</strong> hit where Vegas strategy would double.</li>
    <li><strong>A-7 vs 3–6:</strong> stand where Vegas strategy would double.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>And without surrender, the usual surrender hands (16 vs 9, 10, ace; 15 vs 10) are played by hitting.</p>

  <h2 id="obo">What about OBO tables?</h2>
  <p>Some European tables, mostly online, use <strong>OBO</strong> (“original bets only”): if the dealer makes blackjack you only lose your original bet and get doubles and splits back. There, strategy is the same as with a hole card — tick “dealer checks for blackjack” in the chart.</p>

  <h2 id="edge">What it costs</h2>
  <p>With typical rules and perfect play the house edge is about <strong>0.6%</strong> — a bit worse than a good Las Vegas six-deck game (about 0.4%), mainly because of the missing hole card and the doubling restriction, but far better than any 6:5 table. New to the game? Start with <a href={routes.howToPlay.en}>how to play blackjack</a>.</p>
</Guide>
